### Employee Database Project using `javax.servlet`, MySQL and Tomcat

I'll give you the complete project using `javax.servlet` instead of `jakarta.servlet`, including GET, POST, PUT and DELETE, the folder structure, database connection, compilation, and running instructions on Windows.

Important: Since this project uses `javax.servlet`, use Apache Tomcat 9. Tomcat 10 and 10.1 use `jakarta.servlet`, so this code will not work on them without changes.

### 1\. Software required

| Software                   | Purpose                         |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| JDK 17                     | Compile and run Java            |
| Apache Tomcat 9            | Run the Servlet web application |
| MySQL Server               | Store employee records          |
| MySQL Connector/J          | Connect Java to MySQL           |
| VS Code or Notepad         | Write the code                  |
| MySQL Workbench (optional) | Execute SQL commands            |

### 2\. Complete folder structure

Assume you extract Tomcat to `C:\apache-tomcat-9.0.xx`. Your project should look like this:

apache-tomcat-9.0.xx

webapps

EmployeeDBProject

`index.html`

WEB-INF

`web.xml`

classes

`EmployeeDBServlet.class`

lib

`mysql-connector-j.jar`

Your Java source file, `EmployeeDBServlet.java`, can be saved in a separate folder such as `C:\JavaProjects\EmployeeDBProject\`. You compile it into `WEB-INF\classes`.

### 3\. Create the MySQL database

Open MySQL Workbench, connect to your local MySQL server, and execute the following SQL.

```sql
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS TEST;

USE TEST;

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS Employees (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    first VARCHAR(50),
    last VARCHAR(50),
    age INT
);

INSERT IGNORE INTO Employees
    (id, first, last, age)
VALUES
    (1, 'Arun', 'Kumar', 22),
    (2, 'Priya', 'Sharma', 21);

SELECT * FROM Employees;
```

Expected result:

| id  | first | last   | age |
| --- | ----- | ------ | --- |
| 1   | Arun  | Kumar  | 22  |
| 2   | Priya | Sharma | 21  |

If the database and table already exist, you do not need to create them again.

### 4\. HTML code — `index.html`

Create `index.html` inside `EmployeeDBProject`.

This page provides forms for all four operations. Because HTML forms natively support only GET and POST, the PUT and DELETE forms send a POST request with a hidden `_method` field, which the servlet uses to select the operation.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Employee Database</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Employee Database</h2>

    <h3>1. View Employees - GET</h3>
    <form action="employees" method="get">
        <input type="submit" value="View Employees">
    </form>

    <hr>

    <h3>2. Add Employee - POST</h3>
    <form action="employees" method="post">
        ID: <input type="number" name="id" required><br><br>
        First Name: <input type="text" name="first" required><br><br>
        Last Name: <input type="text" name="last" required><br><br>
        Age: <input type="number" name="age" required><br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Add Employee">
    </form>

    <hr>

    <h3>3. Update Employee - PUT</h3>
    <form action="employees" method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="PUT">

        Employee ID: <input type="number" name="id" required><br><br>
        First Name: <input type="text" name="first" required><br><br>
        Last Name: <input type="text" name="last" required><br><br>
        Age: <input type="number" name="age" required><br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Update Employee">
    </form>

    <hr>

    <h3>4. Delete Employee - DELETE</h3>
    <form action="employees" method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="DELETE">

        Employee ID: <input type="number" name="id" required><br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Delete Employee">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

